Day 9 – Customer Churn Prediction using XGBoost

Objective

The objective of Day 9 was to develop a customer churn prediction model using the XGBoost algorithm. The model predicts whether a customer is likely to stop purchasing based on historical transaction behavior, enabling businesses to implement customer retention strategies.

Dataset Overview

The cleaned retail datasets (2009–2010 and 2010–2011) were imported and merged into a single dataset. Customer-level features were generated by aggregating transaction data for each customer.

Dataset Features

The churn prediction dataset includes the following features:

- Customer ID
- Total Orders
- Total Quantity
- Total Spending
- Last Purchase Date
- Recency
- Churn Label

These features were used to train the XGBoost classification model.

Data Preparation

Customer-level statistics were created by grouping transaction records using Customer ID. Features such as Total Orders, Total Quantity, Total Spending, and Recency were calculated. Customers with a Recency value greater than 90 days were labeled as churned.

Feature Selection

The following features were selected as model inputs:

- Total Orders
- Total Quantity
- Total Spending
- Recency

The target variable was the Churn label.

Train-Test Split

The dataset was divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 ratio. The training data was used to build the model, while the testing data was used to evaluate prediction performance.

XGBoost Model Development

An XGBoost Classifier was developed with suitable hyperparameters, including the number of estimators, learning rate, and maximum tree depth. The model was trained to classify customers as churn or non-churn.

Model Evaluation

The performance of the model was evaluated using:

- Accuracy Score
- Classification Report (Precision, Recall, and F1-Score)

These metrics measured the effectiveness of the churn prediction model.

Model Interpretation

SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) was used to interpret the XGBoost model. Summary plots and feature importance visualizations were generated to identify the most influential factors contributing to customer churn.

Customer Risk Prediction

The trained model predicted churn status and churn probability for every customer. Customers with a churn probability greater than 0.70 were identified as high-risk customers requiring immediate retention efforts.

Prediction Export

The final customer churn predictions were exported as a CSV file named "customer_churn_predictions.csv" for future business analysis and customer relationship management.

Key Findings

• Successfully merged the cleaned retail datasets.
• Generated customer-level behavioral features.
• Created churn labels based on customer recency.
• Trained an XGBoost classification model for churn prediction.
• Evaluated the model using Accuracy Score and Classification Report.
• Explained model predictions using SHAP feature importance analysis.
• Predicted churn probability for every customer.
• Identified high-risk customers for retention strategies.
• Exported churn prediction results for future business use.

Conclusion

Day 9 focused on developing a customer churn prediction system using the XGBoost machine learning algorithm. Customer purchasing behavior was transformed into meaningful features, enabling accurate churn prediction. SHAP analysis improved model interpretability by highlighting the factors influencing customer churn. The generated churn probabilities and high-risk customer list provide valuable insights for customer retention, personalized marketing, and business decision-making.